%U A400560 10884681848556977553,1068696018311293300531,113240941257325291300261,12880497859764078557558177,1565366413807935574346717525
%N A400560 Number of acyclic digraphs A on n labeled nodes together with node sets S and R such that adding a new node h with edges from h to every node of S and from every node of R to h yields a strongly connected digraph that is reducible as a flow graph rooted at every node.
%C A400560 The 'flat core' of everywhere-reducible strong digraphs: equivalently a(n) is the number of pairs (D, h) with D strongly connected on n+1 nodes, reducible from every node, and D - h acyclic, divided by n+1.
%C A400560 The exact recurrence follows from the flat master theorem: such a D is reducible from every node iff for every node x != h the edges from the dominance cone of x (its dominator-tree subtree) into the SCC of h in D - x target at most one vertex. The resulting grammar -- a dominator forest of cones, one focus target per cone, acyclic sibling foci, each cone entered directly or by at least two sibling foci, full reachability -- yields the recurrence below, with the striking collapse that the cone content g(k) equals 2*a(k-1).
%C A400560 The digraphs flat at every root are exactly the directed Hamiltonian cycles.
%D A400560 M. S. Hecht and J. D. Ullman, Flow graph reducibility, SIAM J. Comput. 1 (1972), 188-202.
%D A400560 M. S. Hecht and J. D. Ullman, Characterizations of reducible flow graphs, J. ACM 21 (1974), 367-375.
%D A400560 R. W. Robinson, Counting digraphs with restrictions on the strong components, in T.-H. Ku (ed.), Combinatorics and Graph Theory '95, World Scientific, Singapore, 1995, pp. 343-354.
%D A400560 R. E. Tarjan, Testing flow graph reducibility, J. Comput. System Sci. 9 (1974), 355-365.
%F A400560 Set a(0) = 1, g(1) = 1 and g(k) = 2*a(k-1) for k >= 2. Let H(x) = Sum_{k>=1} k*g(k)*x^k/k!, and let T(z) be the formal power series satisfying T(z) = z*(2*exp(T(z))-1-T(z)). Then A(x) = Sum_{n>=0} a(n)*x^n/n! = exp(T(H(x))); hence a(n) = n!*[x^n] exp(T(H(x))).
%F A400560 Equivalently, a(n) = Sum_{j=0..n} B(n,j)*W(j), where B(n,j) = n!*[x^n] H(x)^j/j! and W(j) = j!*[z^j] exp(T(z)).
%o A400560 (Python) everywhere_reducible_sequence("flat_everywhere_reducible", 20) # See Python link in A400123 for program code.
%Y A400560 Cf. A003030, A052873.
